      "text": "To determine the area difference between the circle and the square, we need to first find the areas of both the square and the circle using their respective dimensions.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the side length of the square.**\n\nThe diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. If \\( s \\) is the side length of the square, then by the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between the side length and the diagonal is:\n\\[\ns\\sqrt{2} = 5\n\\]\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nThe area of the square is:\n\\[\ns^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the radius of the circle.**\n\nThe diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the difference in area between the circle and the square.**\n\nThe area of the square is 12.5 square inches, and the area of the circle is \\( 6.25\\pi \\) square inches. The difference in area is:\n\\[\n6.25\\pi - 12.5\n\\]\nUsing the approximation \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14159 \\):\n\\[\n6.25\\pi \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14159 = 19.6349625\n\\]\nSo, the difference in area is:\n\\[\n19.6349625 - 12.5 = 7.1349625\n\\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n7.1\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
