      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find out by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we need to calculate the areas of both shapes and then find the difference between them.\n\nFirst, let's determine the side length of the square. The diagonal of a square with side length \\( s \\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\ns\\sqrt{2} = 5\n\\]\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nThe area of the square is given by:\n\\[\ns^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's determine the radius of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of the circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\nUsing the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14\\), we get:\n\\[\n6.25\\pi \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14 = 19.625 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square:\n\\[\n19.625 - 12.5 = 7.125\n\\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n7.1\n\\]\nThus, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
