      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, let's find the length of the side of the square. Since the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of a side:\n\n\\[ \\text{side} = \\frac{\\text{diagonal}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\nNext, let's find the area of the square. The area of a square is given by the square of the length of its side:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of the square} = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\]\n\nNow, let's find the radius of the circle. Since the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius is:\n\n\\[ \\text{radius} = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\]\n\nThe area of a circle is given by the formula \\( \\pi r^2 \\). So, the area of the circle is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of the circle} = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\approx 19.6 \\]\n\nFinally, let's find the difference in area between the circle and the square:\n\n\\[ \\text{Difference in area} = 19.6 - 12.5 \\approx 7.1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
